    ```bash
    just test -p codex-app-server-protocol
    ```
+
+
+### Thread-scoped MCP reload
+
+`thread/mcpServer/reload` accepts `{"threadId":"<loaded-thread-id>"}` to plan
+and queue an MCP reload only for that exact loaded thread. Invalid, unknown or
+unloaded selectors fail without falling back to a global reload. The response
+returns `{"threadId":"<loaded-thread-id>","queued":true}` for queue admission; callers must separately observe the selected resources and
+preserve goal/budget state. The method does not restart the shared app-server or
+load another conversation. Legacy `config/mcpServer/reload` remains unchanged.
+
+A request with `"dryRun":true` only validates the loaded target and returns the
+exact `threadId` with `"queued":false`. It does not reload config or MCP clients.
+Clients can use this read-only probe to distinguish this scoped capability from
+older servers before admitting a refresh. Unknown fields are rejected. A missing
+or invalid selector never invokes the legacy global operation.
+
+For completion proof, `mcpServerStatus/list` with `runtimeOnly: true` requires an
+exact loaded `threadId` and reads its existing MCP manager. It never creates a
+temporary connection from the latest file configuration. Omission preserves the
+legacy config-inspection behavior. The scoped API and explicit runtime inspection apply an already
+admitted pending refresh without starting a model turn; legacy runtime reads
+keep their previous behavior. In-flight steps retain
+their previous snapshot until they finish.
